          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been getting heartburn most evenings.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: the risk check returned \"emergency,\" so act on this first.** If you have chest pain, pressure or tightness, or discomfort that spreads to your arm, neck or jaw, especially with breathlessness, sweating or nausea, call emergency services now. Do not drive yourself. Heartburn and heart pain can feel alike, and a history of reflux does not rule out a heart problem (Emergency guidance, red-flag-chest-pain; General reference, GERD).\n\nThe emergency result was triggered partly by the red-flag terms in your question, not by anything you told me you have. If none of those symptoms apply, you still should not wait on heartburn that keeps coming back. Contact a doctor soon. Get help promptly for difficulty or pain when swallowing, vomiting blood or coffee-ground vomit, black tarry stools, or weight loss you cannot explain (Emergency guidance, red-flag-gastrointestinal-bleeding; General reference, GERD).\n\n**Body systems involved:** Your description points to one system, the gastrointestinal (digestive) system. The search also listed other conditions, such as hypothyroidism, osteoarthritis and migraine. The material I retrieved does not link those to heartburn, so I would not read anything into them.\n\n**Questions to take to a clinician:**\n- Is this gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), where stomach acid flows back into the gullet? The general reference covers it, including night-time symptoms and lifestyle steps such as raising the head of the bed, changing portion sizes and stopping smoking. Any medicine should be chosen by a clinician.\n- Do you need tests? The reference notes that ongoing symptoms may call for further assessment, such as a camera test of the gullet.\n- If you have tiredness or pallor along with heartburn, ask whether a blood count and iron check is needed. The reference says iron deficiency in an adult who is not menstruating heavily or pregnant should prompt a search for bleeding in the digestive tract.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
